    "markdown": "---\nengine: julia\nauthor: \"Ernest Orlowski\"\nformat:\n  html:\n    code-fold: true\n    \n\n---\n\n# Statistical Inference Concepts\n\n## 5.1 Random Sample & Distributions of Statistics\n\n::: {#2 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nusing Distributions, Random, Plots;\npyplot();\n\nRandom.seed!(42)\n\nλ = 1 / 4.5\nexpDist = Exponential(1 / λ)\nn, N = 10, 10^6\n\nmeans = Array{Float64}(undef, N)\nvars = Array{Float64}(undef, N)\n\nfor i in 1:N\n    data = rand(expDist, n)\n    means[i] = mean(data)\n    vars[i] = var(data)\nend\n\nprintln(\"Actual mean: \", mean(expDist), \"\\nMean of sample means: \", mean(means))\n\nprintln(\"Actual variance: \", var(expDist), \"\\nMean of sample variances: \", mean(vars))\n\nstephist(means, bins=200, c=:blue, normed=true, label=\"Histogram of Sample Means\")\nstephist!(vars, bins=600, c=:red, normed=true, label=\"Histogram of Sample Variances\", xlims=(0, 40), ylims=(0, 0.4), xlabel=\"Statistic value\", ylabel=\"Density\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n```\nActual mean: 4.5\nMean of sample means: 4.4997684419391275\nActual variance: 20.25\nMean of sample variances: 20.24509570642829\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" />\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.callout-note collapse=\"true\"}\nAfter calculating the sample mean and variance, it can be seen that the estimated expected value of the simulated data is a good approximation to the mean and variance parameters of the underlying exponential distribution. That is, for an exponential distribution with rate $\\lambda$, the mean is $\\lambda^{-1}$ and the variance is $\\lambda^{-2}$. The histograms of the sample means and sample variances are using 200 & 600 bins, respectively.\n:::\n\n## 5.2 Sampling from Normla Populations\n\nIts assumed (*normality assumption*) that the distribution $F(x; \\theta)$ is a normal distribution and hence $\\theta = (\\mu, \\sigma^{2})$. The distribution of the random variables $\\bar{X}$ and $\\mathit{S}^{2}$ as well as transformations of them are well known. The three relationships play a key role:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\bar{X} ~ \\text{Normal}(\\mu, \\sigma^{2}) \\\\\n    =& (n - 1)\\mathit{S}^{2} \\div \\sigma^{2} ~ \\chi^{2}_{n - 1} \\\\\n    =& \\text{T} \\def \\frac{\\bar{X}-\\mu}{\\mathit{S} \\div \\sqrt{n}} ~ \\tau_{n - 1} \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n",
